why: the last site the 2026-09-08 change still reaches.
  `grant-unenforced` found that `okay.Effect` extending
  `Direct.Effect` made two GREEN tests vacuous — they would have
  passed with `provide` deleted. A sweep of the test tree for explicit
  coloring grants found exactly one site left:

    TestDirectAuto.scala:34  given Effect[[X] =>> Reader[Int, X]] with {}

  `Reader derives okay.Effect`, so that grant is redundant — proved
  already by the fourth test `grant-unenforced` added, which colors a
  Reader block with nothing provided.

what: find out whether the test around it still tests anything.

  It is named "operations color via the Effect marker", and if it
  passes with the marker deleted then it does not test the marker; it
  tests that Reader colors, which is a different claim and one that
  now holds for free.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  Delete line 34 and run. Two outcomes, two different fixes:

  a) it still passes -> the test is vacuous. Repair it the way
     `TestEffectProvide` was repaired: move it to a signature that
     does NOT declare itself an effect, so the marker is required
     again and the name is true. Do NOT simply delete the line and
     leave the test — that removes the redundancy and keeps the lie.
  b) it FAILS -> the grant is doing real work I have not understood
     (most likely the partially-applied `[X] =>> Reader[Int, X]` is
     not what `derives` produces for a two-parameter enum). Then line
     34 stays, my reading of `grant-unenforced`'s fourth test was too
     broad, and THAT is the finding — it would mean `derives Effect`
     covers less than I said in the changelog, which is worth a
     correction.

predict: (a). Recorded so being wrong is visible. If it is (b) I owe
  a correction to the grant-unenforced changelog entry, which states
  the coverage more broadly than one test proves.
